  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nThere could be isolated thunderstorms this afternoon across eastern \nOH. A low to mid level northwesterly flow will transport moisture \noff Lake Michigan and interact with instability over eastern Ohio to \ndevelop th CAPE which is how these thunderstorms will develop \nthunderstorms. These storms will not be very strong with minimal low-\nlevel wind shear. Some dry mid-level air could in a damaging wind \npotential across Muskingum and Coshocton counties for a brief \nwindow late afternoon through early evening. The severe \npotential appears low at this time. \n\nThe front associated with this morning's showers will stall across \nsouthern PA/southern OH.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nTonight, a ridge will begin to build into the region as the front \nremains stalled to the south. By Thursday evening, the boundary will \nbegin move northward across the region as a warm front. This will \nbring the chance for scattered showers and thunderstorms. Upper \nsupport and moisture is limited, though a few showers and isolated \nthunderstorms will be possible with weak ascent along the front. \n\nA quasi zonal flow is expected to set up across much of the CONUS by \nSaturday. A shortwave embedded in the flow, and a surface low/cold \nfront, is expected to approach and cross the Upper Ohio Valley \nregion Friday night into Saturday. More widespread showers are \nexpected as this system tracks across the area. There could also be \na few thunderstorms, though instability is expected to be limited \n(ML CAPE <500 J/kg) with minimal shear as well. Some uncertainty \nexists in how fast the surface low and cold front will move east of \nthe area on Saturday. If the faster scenarios were to happen, POPs \ncould be further reduced in the afternoon. Generally dry weather \nreturns Sunday and Monday before a stronger trough and surface cold \nfront approaches and crosses the region Monday night into Tuesday.\n\nTemperatures are expected to warm Thursday and Friday, with readings \ngenerally 10-15 degrees above average. More seasonable temperatures \nare then expected for the weekend with the passage of the cold\nfront.",
